<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II, Count of Flanders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1330">1330</a>–<a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Louis of Male · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-dampierre">House of Dampierre</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II, known as Louis of Male, was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> from 1346 to 1384 and also count of Nevers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>. He steered between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and Mechelen from Brabant, and faced a long revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, which French help defeated at Roosebeke in 1382. The marriage of his heiress Margaret to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, brought Flanders into the Burgundian state on his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders.jpg" alt="Louis II, Count of Flanders" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis de Male (1330-1384; comte de Flandre). Autorité émettrice de monnaie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Pays-Bas,_Flandre,_Louis_II_de_Male,_royal_-_btv1b11341332k_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1369 Margaret of Male, heiress of Count <b>Louis of Male</b>, married Philip the Bold, duke of Burgundy and brother of Charles V of France, and on Louis’s death in 1384 Flanders became part of the Burgundian State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…death of Duke John III in 1355 without a male heir provoked the War of the Brabantine Succession: <b>Louis of Male</b>, count of Flanders, defeated the Brabanters at the Battle of Scheut in August 1356 and took Brussels, until a small band under Everard t'Serclaes recaptured the city…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Male, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.28&amp;year=1365" aria-label="Male on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4624 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis II, Count of Flanders"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5074 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5074 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.21,3.28&amp;year=1365">What was here in 1365? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
